WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.880
In 1692, Richard
Coman told a Salem

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:04.370
court about a night no one else

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.680
could see. The room was locked.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:08.180
A light was burning.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.370
His wife lay beside him.

00:00:10.831 --> 00:00:12.840
Then, he said, Bridget
Bishop entered.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:14.990
She lay across his
chest and pressed

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.680
him down. He could not speak.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.220
He could not move—not
even enough to

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:20.140
wake his wife.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.210
Eight days after Coman
gave that testimony,

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:24.460
Bishop was hanged.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:26.820
His statement did
not create the case

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.880
against her. It
joined a much larger

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.960
prosecution. We cannot
diagnose his night across

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.000
three centuries. The
record reveals what Salem

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.750
did with it: a private
experience entered

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.750
a courtroom and
attached itself to a

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.600
living woman. How does
a private experience

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:43.680
become public truth?

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.200
And can the story around it change

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.140
the next night?

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.460
Coman's pattern is
stark: a familiar room,

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.260
waking awareness, a
body that will not

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.860
answer, pressure
on the chest, and a

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:57.600
presence that seems
to have arrived.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.460
Salem had no language of REM sleep

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:01.560
or muscle atonia.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.739
It had another language
ready: witchcraft.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.000
But Salem did not
invent the pattern.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.120
In 1781, Henry Fuseli
painted The Nightmare.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.410
A woman lies thrown back across a

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.500
bed. A squat figure sits on her

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.480
chest. Behind the
curtain, a horse stares

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.800
into the room with
impossible white eyes.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.720
The painting became
iconic because it gave

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:24.680
the night a body.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:28.820
Across Europe, the old
mare—or mara—rode and

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:29.760
pressed the sleeper.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:32.700
Christian tradition
imagined the incubus.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.840
In Japan, kanashibari
meant being bound as

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:36.760
if by metal.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.860
In Newfoundland, the
visitor became the Old

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.020
Hag. Elsewhere, pressure
and presence were

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.280
understood through jinn.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.120
These are parallels,
not a demon family

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.340
tree. What repeats
is simpler: the room

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.940
is known, the body
is trapped, something

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.480
approaches, and pressure
gains intention.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.430
Perhaps the body produces
an experience that

00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.520
demands a cause.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.220
But once a culture
supplies that cause,

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:04.180
the next sleeper
does not enter the

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:07.340
dark empty-handed. That
is where folklorist

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.949
David Hufford reversed
the usual question.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:13.040
In Newfoundland, he studied
people who described

00:02:13.049 --> 00:02:14.760
the pattern before
they knew the Old

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:16.200
Hag story that named it.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.640
Paralysis. Pressure.
Presence first.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:21.130
The name later.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.980
The experience could
come before the story.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:26.170
Culture did not need to invent the

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.400
event. It could give
a recurring human

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.320
state a face, a motive, and a

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.300
memory. Then that
interpretation could wait

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.140
beside the next bed.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.340
Imagine two people
waking into the same

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.420
paralysis. One knows
the state will pass.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:42.680
The other expects a
being that attacks

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:43.620
in the night.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.350
The body may begin in the same

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.820
place. Does the night end there?

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.430
Baland Jalal and Devon
Hinton compared specific

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.260
samples in Egypt and Denmark.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.500
These were not portraits
of two countries.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:58.420
They were groups whose
reports revealed a

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.890
striking association.
Supernatural explanations,

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.100
especially jinn, were
more common in the

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:07.510
Egyptian samples;
physiological explanations

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.350
were more common in
the Danish sample.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:12.420
Egyptian participants
also reported greater

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:15.280
fear, longer immobility,
and more lifetime

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:18.700
episodes. Within the
Egyptian group, supernatural

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.420
attribution was associated
with greater fear

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:22.600
and longer paralysis.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.120
Science makes the mystery
more precise: meaning

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.360
and bodily distress
can become entangled.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:30.340
Fear can fracture sleep.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.260
Fractured sleep can make
another episode more

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.560
likely. The episode can
confirm the expectation.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.420
The next night arrives
already charged.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.540
Fear. Sleep. Episode.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.740
Expectation. The story
has entered the loop.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:47.720
Return to Richard Coman.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:51.280
His testimony contains
wakefulness, immobility,

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:52.769
pressure, and presence.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.250
Salem supplied a
name, a cause, and

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.180
a court willing to hear it.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.990
The experience moved
outward into testimony;

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.100
the testimony returned
to the community as

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:05.520
reality. So what comes first?

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:08.040
For one person,
perhaps the EXPERIENCE.

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:11.420
For a culture,
EXPERIENCE and STORY may

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:12.820
already be braided together.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.500
Every night meets
images that are waiting.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.340
Every account changes
what the next person

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.240
expects. Which carries
more power into the

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:24.240
next night: EXPERIENCE, or STORY?

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:26.740
Leave one word below.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:28.120
The body may open the door.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:30.300
But the story decides
what is waiting

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:31.240
on the other side.
